## Installing

The environment is narrow and three of its pins exist only to hold back the modern world.
Removing any of them breaks things **silently or confusingly**, so all three have their reason
written beside them in `requirements.txt`.

### Primary: uv

```bash
UV_HTTP_TIMEOUT=3600 uv sync --locked
```

`uv.lock` is the exact resolution this package was verified under. `--locked` refuses to
re-resolve, so a newer uv cannot quietly substitute a different graph.

**`UV_HTTP_TIMEOUT=3600` is mandatory, not defensive.** uv's default is 30 s, which is shorter
than a ~780 MB torch wheel takes on a slow link. It fails as `operation timed out (Connect)`,
which reads like an unreachable host and is not.

### Fallback: pip

```bash
python3.11 -m venv .venv
.venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt
```

`requirements.txt` carries its own `--extra-index-url` and `-f` lines, because a plain
requirements file cannot otherwise express this environment: torch comes from
`download.pytorch.org/whl/cu124` and the three compiled PyG extensions come from a *flat
index* at `data.pyg.org`.

### Non-negotiable, on every path

| pin | why |
|---|---|
| **Python 3.11 exactly** | the PyG extension wheels are built per (python, torch, cuda) triple |
| **`torch==2.6.0+cu124`** | graphium declares *no* torch dependency, so an unpinned resolve replaces it and orphans the compiled extensions |
| **`setuptools<81`** | graphium pins `torchmetrics<0.11`, which imports `pkg_resources`; setuptools 81 deprecated it, 84 removed it |
| **`scipy<1.14`** | graphium's featurizer hands a float16 adjacency matrix to `scipy.sparse.coo_matrix`; scipy ≥ 1.15 rejects float16 |
| **graphium 2.4.7 + minimol 1.3.4** | the trunk is rebuilt through graphium's own loaders; `trunk.py` relies on private APIs that carry no stability promise |

### CUDA, and running without one

The pins target **CUDA 12.4**, which covers sm_80/86/90 (A100, A6000, H100/H200). If your CUDA
differs, change the `+pt26cu124` suffix on torch and the `+pt26cu124` suffix on all three PyG
extensions **together**, along with the `-f` index line — they must agree with each other and
with your driver.

**CPU works.** Verified {cpu_verified}: the full load → encode path runs on CPU under this same
cu124 install and is deterministic there (repeating a batch gives max|Δ| = 0.0). Pass
`device="cpu"`, or just let `device=None` fall back when no GPU is present. A dedicated
CPU-only wheel set (`+pt26cpu`) was **not** tested; if you have a GPU-less machine, the
cu124 install above is the tested route.

### Then, before anything else

```bash
python verify_install.py            # add `cpu` or `cuda` to force a device
```

This is not a smoke test. minimol's own loader uses `load_state_dict(..., strict=False)`, so a
version skew that renames one parameter produces a **partly randomly initialised trunk with no
error** — a model that runs, returns correctly-shaped vectors, and is worthless.
`verify_install.py` compares numbers against the shipped fixture, which key names cannot fake.
Expect `max|Δ|` around 0 on CPU and ~1e-6 on GPU, against a tolerance of 1e-4.

